<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sukkalmah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sukkalmah, meaning grand regent, was the title of the supreme ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukkalmah-period">Sukkalmah period</a>. The holder stood at the head of a tripartite hierarchy with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukkal">sukkal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a> and Susa. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a> describe it as a sophisticated constitutional innovation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sukkalmah.jpg" alt="Sukkalmah" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enyavar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Near_East_1600BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukkal">sukkal</a>-mah, great sukkal, first appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> in an inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urukagina">Urukagina</a>, with a meaning close to prime minister or grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. It remained in use in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, where the sukkalmahs reported directly to the king, and in that form it was adopted by the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>. Siwe-palar-huppak also called himself likawe rishakki, which may be the Elamite rendering of the title, risha being Elamite for great. The Sukkalmah or Epartid dynasty, c. 1900 to c. 1500 BCE, took its name from the title, though not all its rulers used it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The creation of the system is generally attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebarat-ii">Ebarat II</a>, but an inscription of the reign of Idattu I calling Kiten-rakittapi the sukkalmah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> suggests an earlier start, with the sukkalmah of Elam subordinate to the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>. Some rulers held other titles first and are thought to have become sukkalmah later in life. The structure is commonly described as a triumvirate with the sukkalmah as the highest authority, but De Graef proposes at least two sukkalmahs, of Anshan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and of Elam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimashki-dynasty">Shimashki</a>, with several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukkal">sukkals</a> and governors beneath them, and Potts cautions that a simple triumvirate leaves out the many other offices recorded.</p><p>Rulers often called themselves the son of the sister of an earlier ruler, above all of Shilhaha, and at least eleven did so. Some scholars, such as Hinz and Vallat, read this as royal incest, but Potts sees in it an avuncular succession, from uncle to nephew, and De Graef calls the incest theory outdated. Where real descent was impossible, the phrase seems to have claimed legitimacy through a fictitious descent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the letters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> the sukkalmah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a> held a prestigious place in international affairs. He mediated between Zimri-Lim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> over disputed border cities, and while Amorite rulers of their rank addressed one another as brothers, they called the sukkalmah father, denoting superior rank. The scribes of Mari were less sure of the Elamite system, often calling the sukkalmah the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukkal">sukkal</a> of Elam, and many letters from him give only his title. The numerous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> documents from the period, above all from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>, make it one of the best documented in Elamite history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sukkalmah dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Sukkalmah period, around 1765 BCE, the <b>sukkalmah</b> Siwe-Palar-Khuppak led a coalition that conquered Eshnunna, and rulers of the stature of Hammurabi of Babylon and Zimri-Lim of Mari addressed him as father, a term denoting superior rank.</blockquote><a href="/empire/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors took the title <b>sukkalmah</b>, 'grand regent', a term borrowed from Sumerian administration, and ruled from Susa over lowland Khuzestan and the highlands of Anshan.</blockquote><a href="/state/sukkalmah-dynasty">Sukkalmah dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
